.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESM-ENABLE-ENCRYPTION 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-esm-enable-encryption \- encrypt the objects written to a bucket
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli esm enable-encryption
.BR \-\-bucket | \-b
.I ERN
[\fB\-\-key\fR|\fB\-k\fR \fIID\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Turns on encryption at rest for a bucket.
.PP
This applies to
.B new uploads and puts only.
From this call onwards, every object written to the bucket is encrypted
with the bucket's EKM key before it reaches the disk, and decrypted on
the way back out. Nothing about using the bucket changes: uploads,
downloads, copies and the checksums and content types reported for
objects all describe the object, not its ciphertext, and a client cannot
tell an encrypted bucket from any other. What changes is what is on the
disk \- the files under the storage directory are no longer the objects,
so a stolen disk, a backup or a filesystem-level copy yields nothing
without the key.
.PP
Objects the bucket
.B already
holds are not touched. They are on disk as they were stored, and they
stay readable, because every object records the key it was written
under rather than being read through whatever the bucket says today. The
answer reports how many such objects there are, which is the number to
act on if the point of enabling encryption was that nothing in the
bucket should be readable off the disk \- copy them through a new bucket
with
.BR euclid-cli-esm-copy-object (1),
or upload them again.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bucket " " \fIERN\fR
Euclid resource name of the bucket. Required.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " " \fIID\fR
ID or ERN of the EKM key to encrypt under. It must exist, belong to the
caller's account and still be usable for encryption \- a revoked key or
one scheduled for deletion is refused here rather than at the first
upload. Optional; without it a key is created for the bucket.
.SH THE KEY
Without
.BR \-\-key ,
a fresh AES\-256 key is created and the bucket is pointed at it, with a
description naming the bucket so it is not an anonymous ID in
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-list-keys (1)
holding somebody's objects. It is an ordinary EKM key from that moment
on:
.BR euclid-cli-ekm (1)
lists it, tags it, revokes it and schedules it for deletion like any
other, and it is published as
.I ekm.key.created
the same way.
.PP
Which means the key is now the bucket's contents. Deleting it with
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-delete-key (1)
makes every object encrypted under it unrecoverable once the pending
window expires \- that window exists precisely so the data can be
migrated first. Revoking it stops the bucket accepting new objects
while leaving everything already in it readable.
.SH ROTATING
Running this again with another
.B \-\-key
rotates the bucket: objects written from then on go under the new key,
and the ones already there keep naming the key they were written under,
so both remain readable. Nothing is re-encrypted, and no object becomes
unreadable by rotating \- but the old key is still holding the old
objects, so it is not the moment to delete it.
.SH WHAT IS REFUSED
.TP
.B A key that cannot encrypt
A key that does not exist, belongs to another account, carries no key
material, or is revoked or pending deletion. Pointing a bucket at one
would leave a bucket that refuses every upload.
.TP
.B A bucket of another account
Buckets are named by ERN, and an ERN from another account is refused
rather than resolved.
.PP
Once encryption is on, an upload into the bucket is refused if the key
has since become unusable, rather than falling back to storing the
object in the clear: a bucket whose objects are encrypted apart from the
ones written while nobody was looking is the outcome this exists to
prevent.
.SH EVENTS
Publishes
.I esm.bucket.modified
carrying the bucket, the key it is now under and the key it was under
before, and \- when a key was created here \-
.I ekm.key.created
for that key. See
.BR euclid-cli-ees (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Encryption is on. The bucket, the key, whether the key was created by
this call and the number of objects the bucket already held are printed
as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, no session, the bucket does not exist or
belongs to another account, or the named key does not exist or cannot be
used for encryption.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli esm enable-encryption \e
    --bucket ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:development:bucket:reports
.fi
.RE
.PP
prints
.PP
.RS
.nf
{"ern":"ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:development:bucket:reports",
 "name":"reports",
 "keyErn":"ern:ekm:eu-central-1:000000000000:key:9f2c...",
 "keyId":"9f2c...","algorithm":"AES-256",
 "keyCreated":true,"existingObjects":2}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Under a key that already exists:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli esm enable-encryption \e
    --bucket ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:development:bucket:reports \e
    --key 9f2c1e40-3b7a-4c19-9a58-1d2e3f4a5b6c
.fi
.RE
